---
import ListItem from "../components/ListItem.astro";
import Panel from "../components/Panel.astro";
---

<Panel>
    <h1>Projects</h1>
    <p>
        Here are some of my featured open-source projects. Click on an item to
        expand more details.
    </p>
    <ListItem title="postgres_named_parameters" subtitle="(Rust library)">
        <p><b>Type:</b> Personal project (library)</p>
        <p><b>Tools:</b> Rust</p>
        <p>
            <b>Overview: </b>A lightweight macro wrapper around the postgres
            crate which gives you the ergonomics of named parameters in your raw
            SQL queries. Under the hood, your named parameters are transformed
            into numbered parameters at compile time.
        </p>
        <p>
            <a href="https://github.com/jmoore34/postgres-named-parameters"
                >View on GitHub</a
            >
        </p><p>
            <a
                href="https://docs.rs/postgres-named-parameters/0.1.0/postgres_named_parameters/"
                >View on docs.rs</a
            >
        </p>
    </ListItem>
    <ListItem title="hanzi-svg" subtitle="(TypeScript library)">
        <p><b>Type:</b> Personal project (library)</p>
        <p><b>Tools:</b> TypeScript</p>
        <p>
            <b>Overview: </b>A TypeScript library to create animated SVGs of
            hanzi (Chinese character) stroke order animations. While a library
            for stroke order animations (<a
                href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/hanzi-writer"
                >hanzi-writer</a
            >) exists, I wanted something more lightweight (no
            <code>requestAnimationFrame()</code>`) to maximize performance for
            my use case (namely, including stroke order animations in my Anki
            flashcard deck). Thus, I studied the SVG spec in order to leverage
            SVG-native animation capabilities and get the bundle size down to
            3kb.
        </p>
        <p>
            <a href="https://github.com/jmoore34/hanzi-svg.js">View on GitHub</a
            >
        </p><p>
            <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/hanzi-svg">View on NPM</a>
        </p>
    </ListItem>
    <ListItem
        title="Deployment Environments for GitLab"
        subtitle="(Browser extension)"
    >
        <p><b>Type:</b> Work project</p>
        <p><b>Tools:</b> JavaScript</p>
        <p>
            <b>Overview: </b>A browser extension to query the status of deployed
            environments and overlay them in the GitLab commits view, obviating
            the need to manually compare which commits are included in each
            environment. After publishing this extension, it was adopted by the
            entire team and is used daily to make standup more efficient.
        </p>
        <p>
            <a href="https://github.com/jmoore34/gitlab-deployment-environments"
                >View on GitHub</a
            >
        </p>
        <p>
            <a
                href="https://chromewebstore.google.com/detail/deployment-environments-f/ihenkkccaaacabdgcendjljnoolncbbg"
                >View Chrome Extension</a
            >
        </p>
        <p>
            <a
                href="https://addons.mozilla.org/en-US/firefox/addon/deployment-environments-gitlab/"
                >View Firefox Extension</a
            >
        </p>
    </ListItem>
    <ListItem title="Quine Relay" subtitle="(Kotlin, C, C++, C#, Lisp, Python)">
        <p><b>Type:</b> Personal project</p>
        <p>
            <b>Tools:</b> Kotlin, Unit Testing (kotest); C, C++, C#, Lisp, Python
        </p>
        <p>
            <b>Overview: </b>A <i>quine</i> is a program that outputs its own exact
            source code. I designed an algorithm, implemented in Kotlin, to deterministically
            create a "quine relay", namely: a Python program that outputs a C program
            that outputs a C++ program that outputs a C# program that outputs a Lisp
            program that outputs the exact original Python program. Each program in
            the cycle works independently and runs without any input, network, expensive
            computation, or disk reads. Moreover, each program in the cycle prints
            colored documentation about how to compile the next program in the cycle.
        </p>
        <p>
            <a href="https://github.com/jmoore34/quine-relay">View on GitHub</a>
        </p>
        <p><a href="https://bit.ly/quinerelay">View live demo</a></p>
    </ListItem>
    <ListItem title="Dice Bot" subtitle="(Rust)">
        <p><b>Type:</b> Personal project</p>
        <p><b>Tools:</b> Rust, Discord bot API</p>
        <p>
            <b>Overview: </b>A Rust-based Discord bot with a hand-written dice
            expression parser (e.g. parsing "4d6r1k3*6"). Configured GitHub
            Actions to automatically build and release Windows, Linux, and MacOS
            binaries.
        </p>
        <p><a href="https://github.com/jmoore34/dicebot/">View on GitHub</a></p>
    </ListItem>
    <ListItem title="Role Assigner" subtitle="(TypeScript/React, Kotlin/Ktor)">
        <p><b>Type:</b> Personal project</p>
        <p><b>Tools:</b> React, Ktor</p>
        <p>
            <b>Overview: </b>Full-stack React application allowing for
            anonymized role assignment using custom WebSocket server. Features
            live, Google Docs-like collaboration, input debouncing, material
            design, and a custom API.
        </p>
        <p>
            <a href="https://github.com/jmoore34/roleassigner">View on GitHub</a
            >
        </p>
    </ListItem>
    <p>Older projects, kept for sentimental value:</p>
    <ListItem title="Web Tuner" subtitle="(C#/Unity)">
        <p><b>Type:</b> Personal project</p>
        <p><b>Tools:</b> React, WebAudio API, Semantic UI</p>
        <p>
            <b>Overview: </b>Progressive Web App (PWA) designed to provide
            "drone notes" for musicians, with the rare feature of playing
            multiple octaves concurrently.
        </p>
        <p>
            <a href="https://github.com/jmoore34/tuner">View on GitHub</a>
        </p><p>
            <a href="https://jmoore34.github.io/tuner/">View live demo</a>
        </p>
    </ListItem>
    <ListItem
        title="Degree Audit Tool"
        subtitle="(TypeScript/React, Python/Flask)"
    >
        <p><b>Type:</b> Senior design project</p>
        <p><b>Tools:</b> React, Flask</p>
        <p>
            <b>Overview: </b>Full-stack React application reimagining how
            students plan and share their degree plans, increasing advising
            efficiency. Students click on classes in a degree flowchart to
            assign color-coded semesters and then can export their degree plan
            as an image. Includes drag-and-drop SHA-256 password-authenticated
            flowchart editor for advisors using custom REST API.
        </p>
        <p>
            <a href="https://github.com/jmoore34/degreeaudit">View on GitHub</a>
        </p>
    </ListItem>
    <ListItem title="ShareCalc" subtitle="(Java/Android)">
        <p><b>Type:</b> Class project</p>
        <p>
            <b>Tools:</b> Android/Java, JUnit 5, UML/Object-Oriented Design
        </p>
        <p>
            <b>Overview: </b>Formally planned scientific, unit, and programmer
            calculator app for Android with emphasis on sharing calculations
            with others. Formalized requirements, sequence diagrams, and class
            diagrams. Handwrote a lexer and bottom-up parser, object-oriented
            evaluation engine, and 92 JUnit tests to allow for computation of
            arbitrarily complex scientific and bitwise expressions. Acted as
            primary IC and team lead, conducting code reviews, setting
            deadlines, and approving PRs.
        </p>
        <p>
            <a href="https://github.com/MayaIsla/3354-Team-Moc"
                >View on GitHub</a
            >
        </p>
    </ListItem>
    <ListItem title="Tobor Herding Game" subtitle="(C#/Unity)">
        <p><b>Type:</b> Class project</p>
        <p><b>Tools:</b> C#/Unity</p>
        <p>
            <b>Overview: </b>3rd-person 3D game. Did most of the coding,
            including movement, camera rigging, AI behavior, game logic, and
            menus.
        </p>
        <p>
            <a href="https://github.com/jmoore34/ToborHerding-Unity"
                >View on GitHub</a
            >
        </p><p>
            <a href="https://jmoore34.github.io/ToborHerding-WebGL/"
                >View live demo</a
            >
        </p>
    </ListItem>
    <ListItem title="Cube Clock" subtitle="(TypeScript/React)">
        <p><b>Type:</b> Personal mini-project</p>
        <p><b>Tools:</b> React, Typescript, SVG, styled-components</p>
        <p>
            <b>Overview: </b>Hue-shifted animated clock PWA featuring SVG cubes
            written from scratch.
        </p>
        <p>
            <a href="https://github.com/jmoore34/cubeclock">View on GitHub</a>
        </p>
        <p>
            <a href="https://jmoore34.github.io/cubeclock/">View live demo</a>
        </p>
        <p>
            <b>Note:</b> For a related mini-project of mine, see <a
                href="https://jmoore34.github.io/binaryclock/">Binary Clock</a
            >
        </p> (<a href="https://github.com/jmoore34/binaryclock">repo</a>)
    </ListItem>
    <ListItem title="IR Chat" subtitle="(C++, C#)">
        <p><b>Type:</b> Personal project</p>
        <p><b>Tools:</b> Embedded C++, C#</p>
        <p>
            <b>Overview: </b>Designed (and constructed) hardware and software to
            allow two offline computers to send and receive data simultaneously
            via infrared beams. Used embedded C++ for the microcontroller and C#
            for the desktop client and GUI.
        </p>
        <p>
            <a href="https://github.com/jmoore34/ir-chat">View on GitHub</a>
        </p>
    </ListItem>
    <ListItem title="4-bit CPU" subtitle="(Schematic)">
        <p><b>Type:</b> Personal project</p>
        <p><b>Tools:</b> Embedded C++, C#</p>
        <p>
            <b>Overview: </b>A 4-bit CPU supporting addition, subtraction,
            comparison, XOR, AND, OR, and NOT instructions. Loading of
            instruction, operand 1, operand 2, and result registers is
            synchronized via a 3-bit timer which feeds into a decoder.
        </p>
        <p>
            <a href="https://jmoore34.github.io/cpu.png">View Schematic</a>
        </p>
    </ListItem>
</Panel>
